\'s another way: In the original quadrilateral, each side can be expressed as a vector. When you project each vertex onto the diagonals, the projections can be written in terms of the components along the diagonals. The new quadrilateral\'s sides are these components.\n\nBut area is calculated as half the determinant of the vectors formed by two adjacent sides. So if the sides of the original quadrilateral are vectors with magnitude and direction, projecting them onto the diagonals would result in scalar components. But area in the original quadrilateral is (1/2)*|s1*s2*sin(phi)|, where phi is the angle between sides. When sides are projected to scalars (components along diagonals), the sin(phi) term would still be present, but the magnitudes are scaled by cos(theta).\n\nWait, not sure. Let me formalize.\n\nLet’s let the vectors representing the sides of the original quadrilateral be **a**, **b**, **c**, **d**.\n\nThe area of the original quadrilateral is (1/2)|(**a** × **b**) + (**)b** × **c**\\)), which for a general quadrilateral in 3D would be more complex, but in 2D, the area is (1/2)| (a_x b_y - a_y b_x ) + (b_x c_y - b_y c_x ) + (c_x d_y - c_y d_x ) + (d_x a_y - d_y a_x ) ). \n\nHowever, if the diagonals are at angle theta, then projecting the vectors onto the diagonals would involve a transformation. If we have a coordinate system where diagonals are x and y axes at angle theta, then the sides of the quadrilateral would be vectors in this plane.\n\nThe feet of the perpendiculars are the projections of the vertices onto the diagonals. So, each vertex projected onto diagonal AC (x-axis) or BD (y-axis). For example, projecting point A onto diagonal BD gives foot P. This projection is equivalent to taking the component of the vector **A** onto the diagonal BD.\n\nWait, perhaps using the formula for projection of a vector onto another vector.\n\nLet’s say vector **A** is a vertex coordinate vector. To project **A** onto diagonal BD, which we can take as the y-axi